From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 00:57:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] tools: merge the two `native/` compat trees into
 `tools/libcompat`

`tools/ctf/native` and `tools/sgs/native` were two spellings of one job:
"compile gate source on a host whose libc is not illumos'". Each grew next to
the tool that first needed it, so between them we had two `native_compat.h`,
two `synch.h`, two `thread.h` and two `native_support.c` -- overlapping,
diverging, and neither of them actually specific to the CTF tools or to the
link-editor. A third consumer would have had to pick one or start a third.

They are now one tree, `usr/src/tools/libcompat/common`:

  * `native_compat.h` is the union. The two files already shared the illumos
    type vocabulary (`boolean_t`, `uchar_t` and friends) and the
    `<sys/isa_defs.h>`/`<sys/ccompile.h>` preamble. From the CTF side it takes
    `_LARGEFILE64_SOURCE`, which `<sys/ctf_api.h>` needs for `off64_t`; from
    the sgs side the `<sys/sysmacros.h>` macros, `auxv_t`, `_INT64_TYPE`,
    `PAGESIZE` and the `NL_*` limits. It also picks up `ARRAY_SIZE` and
    `__DECONST`, which gate source reaches through headers that are too
    kernel-flavoured to stage whole.

  * `synch.h` and `thread.h` are likewise unions: the CTF half brought
    condition variables, `LOCK_ERRORCHECK`, `mutex_enter()`/`mutex_exit()`
    and `thr_create()`/`thr_join()`; the sgs half brought reader/writer locks,
    thread-specific data and `thr_main()`. Where both defined `mutex_init()`
    the CTF one wins, because it honours the lock type rather than ignoring it.

  * `native_support.c` merges both. `assfail()` is **weak**: libconv links
    `cmd/sgs/common/assfail.c` as well as this file, so a strong definition
    would be a duplicate symbol for every sgs consumer. Weak lets the copy
    that ships with the sources win and leaves this as the fallback.

  * `sys/` gathers all six shims -- `elf.h` from the CTF side, `cmn_err.h`,
    `mman.h`, `systeminfo.h`, `utsname.h` and `varargs.h` from the sgs side.

Consumers no longer spell out their own `-I`/`-include`. `Makefile.tools`
defines `COMPAT_CPPFLAGS` and `COMPAT_SUPPORT_SRC` once, and
`Makefile.ctf.native`, `sgs/Makefile.com`, `ctf/libctf/Makefile.com` and
`sgs/libconv/Makefile` use them. Naming it in one place is the point: the
duplication this removes started as two makefiles each spelling out a path.

No functional change intended for either existing consumer -- every addition
each side gains from the other is `#ifndef`-guarded or a new symbol.
